@node Multi-target configuration
@section Tags and captured configurations

@code{--enable-multi-target=@var{triple},@var{triple},@dots{}} names
the secondary targets; @code{--enable-multi-target} alone (or
@code{=all}) enables every port that declares itself ready (see the
porting checklist below).  Each enabled target --- the primary
included, and always first --- receives a @dfn{tag}, the CPU component
of its triple: a build for x86_64 with aarch64 and m68k secondaries
works with the tags @code{x86_64}, @code{aarch64} and @code{m68k}.

For every tag, @file{configure} re-sources @file{config.gcc} in a
pristine probe subshell with that target's triple and captures the
resulting variables --- @code{tm_file}, @code{md_file},
@code{extra_objs}, the configured assembler override and so on --- into
@file{mt-config-@var{tag}.sh} in the build directory.  Everything the
build later does per target is driven by these captures; the host
configuration itself remains exactly the primary's.

@node Multi-target union surface
@section One layout for every backend

Core code is compiled once, against the primary's headers plus a set
of routed macros (@pxref{Multi-target host routing}).  Everything
whose @emph{layout} depends on a target macro must instead be laid out
identically for every backend of the build; the mechanisms are:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@dfn{Union machine modes}.  The mode enumeration is the union of every
enabled target's modes, numbered once for the whole build.  Each
target contributes a table blob holding its values at the union
numbering, installed at activation into the runtime arrays
(@code{mode_precision}, @code{mode_size}, the wider/inner/complex
links, @code{class_narrowest_mode}, @code{real_format_for_mode},
@dots{}).  Value rows of modes a target lacks carry the mode's
intrinsic properties --- scalar precision and size are facts about the
mode, not about a target, and ``infinite precision'' idioms such as
@code{MAX_MODE_INT} folds read them --- while link rows stay
per-target, so mode iteration never reaches a foreign mode.  A
dedicated @code{mode_present} predicate answers whether the active
target has a mode at all.

@item
@dfn{Superset maxima}.  Structures sized by a target constant use the
maximum over the enabled targets, collected into @file{mt-maxima.h}:
@code{MAX_HARD_REGISTERS} and @code{MAX_REG_CLASSES} size register
sets and tables (iteration bounds remain the active target's runtime
counts), @code{MT_MAX_CUMULATIVE_ARGS_SIZE} pads the argument cursor
inside @code{struct function}, and the @code{operand_alternative}
register-filter bitfields take the widest layout.  Host-wide constants
that shape types, such as @code{NUM_POLY_INT_COEFFS}, are likewise the
maximum.

@item
@dfn{No conditional layouts}.  A declaration shared across surfaces
must not change shape under a target's preprocessor conditions.  The
canonical example is @code{global_rtl_index}: single-target builds
collapse the frame, argument and hard-frame pointer slots when a
port's macros make them aliases, but a multi-target build keeps every
slot distinct in @emph{every} translation unit --- target code
included --- and establishes the aliasing at runtime instead, when
@code{init_emit_regs} stores one rtx through every alias.  A backend
translation unit that collapsed the enum by its own macros would read
every member of @code{struct target_rtl} behind the array through a
shifted layout.
@end itemize

@node Multi-target blobs
@section Per-target generated code and renames

Each enabled target gets a full set of generated backend files, built
in @file{mt-@var{tag}/} inside the target's own header context:
target-independent generators are built once and run once per target
(with the target's @file{.md} file and insn conditions), while
generators whose own build depends on target headers
(@command{genmodes}, @command{gencondmd}) are built per target in
@file{build/mt-@var{tag}/}.  Pattern elision through
@command{gencondmd} therefore stays exactly per-target.

Symbol namespacing is source-level: every generator knows the external
symbols it emits, and under @option{--mt-prefix=mt_@var{tag}_} it
prepends a rename prologue (@code{#define gen_addsi3
mt_x86_64_gen_addsi3} and so on) to its output headers, included by
both the generated translation units and the port's own objects
through the per-target @file{tm.h} chain.  Port exports are already
prefixed by port convention.  The @code{mt-check-symbols} build rule
runs an @command{nm} audit asserting that no global symbol collides
across blobs and that a blob's exports are its descriptor surface.

Each secondary also receives a plugin-style @command{gengtype -P} run
over its @code{target_gtfiles}: the resulting root tables are
registered at activation, and the port-owned @code{machine_function}
markers join the backend descriptor (@pxref{Multi-target activation}).

@node Multi-target descriptor
@section What describes a backend

@code{struct target_backend} (@file{target-backend.h}) is the stable
contract between a blob and the compiler.  It carries the canonical
triple, the target hook vectors (@code{targetm} and the common hooks),
the generated entry points (instruction data, recognizer, extractor,
splitters, peepholes, optab initialization, attribute and DFA tables),
the option tables and state images, the mode table blob, the register
information read from the port's macros, and a set of @dfn{captured
callables} for target macros host code must evaluate: the
@code{FUNCTION_VALUE} and @code{PRINT_OPERAND} families, reload
classes and costs, frame offsets, @code{EPILOGUE_USES},
@code{CONSTANT_ADDRESS_P}, @code{PIC_OFFSET_TABLE_REGNUM} and others.

A capture is a function compiled inside the target's header context
that evaluates the port's macro at each call, so macros defined in
terms of runtime state (@code{flag_pic}, reload progress, the port's
own predicates) behave exactly as they do in a single-target build.
Two units host them: @file{target-register-tables.cc} reads the
register-file macros without @file{rtl.h}, and
@file{target-backend-def.cc} --- which has full RTL context ---
captures the rest and defines the descriptor object itself.  The
descriptor is compiled once per target under the target's renames; the
primary's copy, @code{default_target_backend}, is the host build's
own.

@node Multi-target activation
@section The registry and the runtime switch

@file{target-registry.cc} holds
@code{target_backend_registry}, the configured target first.
@code{activate_target_backend} makes a backend the one the compiler
addresses; it runs before option decoding --- from the
@option{-fmulti-target=} prescan in @code{toplev::main}, which
activates the primary explicitly when nothing is selected, because an
earlier invocation in the same process (libgccjit) may have activated
another target.

Installation swaps the pointers core code reads --- the hook vectors,
option tables, register tables and scalars, the captured callables ---
copies the target's mode tables into the runtime arrays (the primary
binds its own tag's blob for this purpose, so switching back reinstalls
values identical to its seed), and re-runs @code{init_reg_sets} from
the just-installed register tables.

Two pieces make repeated activation in one process safe:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Every backend's GTY roots are registered the first time it becomes
active and stay registered.  A port's rooted statics are static
storage, always valid to mark, and whatever they still reference from
an earlier activation must survive until the target is activated
again.

@item
Because those roots keep another target's objects reachable, a
garbage collection can encounter a foreign @code{struct function}.
@code{cfun->machine} is laid out by the port that allocated it, so
@code{allocate_struct_function} records each
@code{machine_function}'s allocating backend and the marker thunks
walk every object with its owner's marker, never the active one's.
@end itemize

@node Multi-target host routing
@section Reading the active target from core code

Host code keeps using the historical macro names; in a multi-target
build @file{defaults.h} redefines them over the active target's
captures --- a scalar member, a captured callable or a descriptor
field.  The flip region is guarded by the canonical exclusion set, and
the guards are load-bearing:

@table @code
@item GENERATOR_FILE
Generator programs are host tools with their own header context.

@item IN_TARGET_CODE
Port code and per-target units read their own macros natively.

@item MT_NATIVE_TARGET_SURFACE, MT_NATIVE_REGISTER_CONSTANTS
The descriptor and registry units bind a target's own tables by their
real names and seed the runtime values from the primary.

@item USED_FOR_TARGET
Target libraries compile @file{tm.h} too and keep raw constants.
@end table

The rule for new work mirrors the original classification: a macro
needs routing if and only if the contents of a
non-@code{IN_TARGET_CODE} object depend on it.  Macros that shape
types take the union-surface mechanisms instead, and macros only port
code reads need nothing at all.

@node Multi-target options
@section Option tables and state under several targets

The option table is two blocks: the common and language rows, in
identical order for every target, then each target's own rows.  A
per-target name-order permutation keeps @code{find_opt}'s binary
search working over the two-block table, so common @code{OPT_*} values
stay build-wide constants.  @code{struct gcc_options} keeps an
identical common prefix and pads the target tail to the largest
enabled target; the save/restore/print/hash entry points for target
option state are per-target functions reached through the descriptor.
Activation installs tables, permutation and defaults image before
anything decodes.

@node Multi-target driver
@section Selection, specs and cross tools

The driver consumes @option{--target=} before spec setup, validates it
against the built-in triples once diagnostics are configured, and
routes the selection to @command{cc1} by prepending
@option{-fmulti-target=} to @code{cc1_spec}.  Each target's spec
strings --- @code{ASM_SPEC}, its @code{EXTRA_SPECS} table, its
@code{DRIVER_SELF_SPECS} and its @code{OPTION_DEFAULT_SPECS} --- are
captured by @file{driver-specs.cc} compiled per tag; under a secondary
selection the capture is installed into @code{static_specs} inside
@code{set_up_specs}, the captured self specs run in the macro's
positions, and the configure-time option defaults are deliberately
@emph{not} run, because @code{do_option_spec} would match generic
names against the primary's configure defaults.

Unrecognized @option{-m} options under a secondary selection pass
through to @command{cc1}, which re-parses them with the active
target's table --- the driver only routes text, so a foreign option is
diagnosed by the target that owns it.  Cross tools resolve in the
order configured override (@code{--with-mt-as-@var{tag}=}), prefixed
@file{@var{triple}-as} in the driver's search directories, then on
@env{PATH}; a compilation that needs an assembler and found none stops
with a clear error, while @option{-S} and @option{-###} need none.
@option{-print-multi-targets} lists the built-in triples, primary
first, and is what the testsuites key on.

@node Multi-target libgccjit
@section Target selection and switching in JIT

@code{gcc_jit_context_set_target} records a triple on the context
(validated against the registry immediately), and playback appends
@option{-fmulti-target=} to the fake argv, so every compilation enters
through the same prescan as @command{cc1}.  One process may compile
for target after target: each compilation activates its target ---
explicitly the primary when the context selects nothing --- and the
switching support in the registry (permanent roots, owner-walked
machine function marking, the primary's own mode-table blob) keeps
repeated and interleaved activations deterministic.  The jit testsuite
asserts determinism byte-for-byte: recompiling after switching away
and back must reproduce the earlier output exactly, for secondary and
default targets alike.

@node Multi-target testing
@section Identity gates and the testsuites

Three properties anchor the series and any future port work:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@dfn{Disabled identity}: a build without @option{--enable-multi-target}
produces byte-identical generated sources and objects.

@item
@dfn{Primary identity}: selecting the primary explicitly produces
output byte-identical to a plain invocation.

@item
@dfn{Golden secondaries}: a secondary's output is instruction-identical
to a real cross compiler built from the same sources.
@end itemize

@file{gcc.dg/multi-target/} exercises selection, driver specs and tool
lookup; @file{lib/target-supports.exp} exposes
@code{check_effective_target_multi_target} (and per-tag variants)
keyed on @option{-print-multi-targets}, so the tests self-skip in
single-target builds.  The jit.dg multi-target tests cover switching,
shared-state bleed (the long double format), context reuse and
threads.

@node Multi-target limitations
@section What is fenced or deferred

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{lto1} is not multi-target; @option{-flto} with a selected
secondary is unsupported.

@item
Precompiled headers do not record target identity; using a PCH across
selections is unsupported.

@item
A secondary's own inserted passes (its @file{@var{port}-passes.def})
are not woven into the pass list; the primary's inserted passes are
gated on the active backend.  Optimization tie-breaks that those
passes would decide can differ from a native cross compiler.

@item
RTL dump cosmetics (register and unspec @emph{names}) print through
the host's tables; the numbers are the active target's.
@end itemize
